//@version=5
// V22 -- Donchian 30/20 long with a VOLATILITY-ADAPTIVE STOP.
//
// ================================ READ THIS BEFORE THE INPUTS ================================
// THIS IS NOT A NEW SIGNAL. The trigger is the same plain Donchian 30 breakout this branch has
// shipped and re-tested a dozen times, and it does NOT beat a random-entry control at these
// lengths. The only thing V22 adds is WHERE THE STOP GOES, and that is the only thing it claims.
//
// It also is NOT a VIX strategy. A VIX file was supplied and it shares ZERO sessions with any
// futures feed here, so it could only be studied against SPX at daily scale. There it CONFIRMED the
// mechanism below and contributed nothing tradeable of its own. See STUDY_V22_VOLATILITY.md.
//
// ================================ THE MECHANISM ================================
// An ATR stop is sized from TRAILING volatility. Volatility MEAN-REVERTS. So when realised vol sits
// LOW in its own distribution, ATR(14) has already contracted and a fixed 2.0N stop is TOO SMALL
// for the excursion the trade is about to make. Measured as median MAE in ATR UNITS, NQ locked block:
//
//   vol percentile   15m MAE p50   15m stop-out   30m MAE p50   30m stop-out
//   0.0 - 0.2            2.09          45.6%          2.18          45.8%
//   0.4 - 0.6            2.03          41.4%          1.61          40.0%
//   0.8 - 1.0            1.24          31.5%          0.98          20.0%
//
// Monotone on four independent columns, and the HELD-OUT block reproduces the research slope almost
// value for value. Heat is 1.8-2.2x larger in the calm bucket. The direction is the opposite of the
// usual advice ("widen stops when volatility is high").
//
// ================================ WHAT IT IS WORTH, LOCKED BLOCK ================================
//   NQ 15m   flat 2.0N   +0.0939 R   PF 1.158        adaptive   +0.1454 R   PF 1.249
//   NQ 30m   flat 2.0N   +0.0873 R   PF 1.156        adaptive   +0.1008 R   PF 1.181
//
// That is a SIZING CORRECTION ON AN EXISTING RULE, worth about +0.09 and +0.03 profit factor. It is
// not an edge and it will not rescue a trigger that has none.
//
// ================================ THE FOUR WAYS IT WAS ATTACKED ================================
// Widening a stop ENLARGES the R denominator, and the widened bucket is the LOSING one -- so a
// policy that only shrinks losses arithmetically would look like an improvement without being one.
//   1  POINTS, where no denominator moves: locked +8.894 -> +11.058 (15m) and +11.227 -> +13.604
//      (30m). Survives. But 30m RESEARCH points FALL (+7.101 -> +4.438) while research R rises --
//      on that one cell the gain is partly the rescaling, and it is not explained away.
//   2  EXIT MIX. A real widening converts stop-outs into channel exits; a rescaling leaves the mix
//      alone. Calm bucket, 15m locked: 42.8% stopped at 2.0N -> 27.1% at 2.5N -> 14.3% at 3.0N,
//      with points per trade rising +0.141 -> +4.464 -> +5.018. It is a mechanism.
//   3  THE THRESHOLD was declared at 0.50, not searched. Sweeping 0.3-0.7 gives a SMOOTH plateau
//      with no spike at 0.5, and every rung beats the flat stop out of sample. Note the two
//      timeframes slope in OPPOSITE directions across it, so do not read a peak into any one value.
//   4  THE NAIVE INVERSE (wide when vol is HIGH) is WORSE THAN FLAT on all six cells tested.
//      Six sign checks, six passes. That is what says the state is the cause.
//
// ================================ THE ORDER MODEL, AND WHY THE STOP ANCHORS WHERE IT DOES ================
// The research engine anchors the stop to the ENTRY BAR'S OPEN. A script cannot: at the moment the
// exit order must be written, the fill price does not exist yet. Placing the exit a bar late leaves
// the ENTRY BAR UNPROTECTED, which STUDY_PINE_PARITY measured at 4.4-13.0% of trades averaging -33
// to -118 points. So this script anchors to the SIGNAL BAR'S CLOSE and places entry and exit
// TOGETHER, which is knowable at order time and leaves no unprotected bar. Measured cost of that
// substitution, NQ: 99.03% (15m) and 99.50% (30m) of trades exit on the IDENTICAL bar, per-trade R
// correlation 0.9935 and 0.9998, locked PF 1.249 -> 1.241 and 1.181 -> 1.182. See v22anchor.py.
//
// ================================ WHAT THE PARITY HARNESS CAUGHT ================================
// `research/v22/v22_parity.py` re-implements THIS SCRIPT'S order model in Python and diffs it
// against the engine, because a Pine port cannot be asserted by reading it. It found a real bug in
// the first draft of this file, which was lint-clean and read correctly: the `lastExitBar` update
// sat BELOW the entry block, so on the bar a trade closed the entry test ran against a stale value
// and the script could re-enter immediately -- 95 trades on 15m and 61 on 30m that the research
// position lock forbids, and they were bad ones. Counting `strategy.closedtrades` rather than
// watching `strategy.position_size[1]` also catches a trade that opens and stops out inside one
// bar, where position_size reads 0 at both closes.
//
// After the fix, against the engine on the same bars:
//   series        percentile rank, ATR, entry channel, exit channel -- all EXACT, 0 disagreements
//   trades        1162 of 1163 (15m) and 664 of 665 (30m); the one missing is open at data end
//   exit bar      100.00% identical on both timeframes
//   correlation   0.999971 (15m) and 0.999983 (30m) on per-trade points
// The residual +4.08 and +4.03 points per trade is exactly the round turn the engine nets and the
// harness does not, which is the check that the fee is the ONLY thing left between them.
//
// ================================ SETTINGS THAT DECIDE WHETHER THE REPORT IS ABOUT THE STRATEGY ====
// TICK ALL THREE "Script execution" / recalculate boxes OFF. If a checkbox changes the report, the
// report is about the checkbox. Every state write below is guarded by barstate.isconfirmed.
// SET COMMISSION AND SLIPPAGE. Research charges the real MNQ stack, $1.44 round turn plus
// bar-dependent slippage. A Commission load of 0.00% means the fills were free and the P&L is not.
// USE AN ACCOUNT LARGE ENOUGH. The broker emulator silently REJECTS unfundable orders.
//
// Research tooling for education and analysis -- not financial advice.

strategy("V22 Adaptive-Volatility Donchian", overlay = true,
     default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
     initial_capital = 100000, pyramiding = 0, calc_on_every_tick = false,
     process_orders_on_close = false, commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_order,
     commission_value = 0.72, slippage = 2)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- inputs
grpS = "Signal"
entryLen = input.int(30, "Donchian entry length", minval = 2, group = grpS)
exitLen  = input.int(20, "Donchian exit length",  minval = 2, group = grpS)
atrLen   = input.int(14, "ATR length",            minval = 1, group = grpS)

grpV = "Adaptive stop"
useAdaptive = input.bool(true, "Scale the stop by the volatility state", group = grpV)
volLen    = input.int(20,  "Realised-vol window (bars)",   minval = 2,  group = grpV)
rankLen   = input.int(250, "Percentile lookback (bars)",   minval = 20, group = grpV)
volThresh = input.float(50.0, "Percentile split", minval = 1.0, maxval = 99.0, step = 1.0,
     group = grpV, tooltip = "Declared at 50, not searched. The 30-70 plateau is smooth.")
stopCalm  = input.float(2.5, "Stop x ATR when vol is CALM (at or below the split)", step = 0.1,
     group = grpV, tooltip = "Wider, because trailing ATR has contracted and understates the heat.")
stopFast  = input.float(1.5, "Stop x ATR when vol is FAST (above the split)", step = 0.1,
     group = grpV)
stopFlat  = input.float(2.0, "Stop x ATR when the adaptive stop is OFF", step = 0.1, group = grpV)

grpT = "Session"
useWindow = input.bool(false, "Restrict entries to a time window", group = grpT,
     tooltip = "OFF by default. Every result quoted above is ALL HOURS. Seven windows were tested " +
     "on a sibling rule and only 08:00-12:00 beat all-hours on both blocks -- which seven looks " +
     "corrects to p 0.168, so a window is a candidate here, not a finding.")
startH = input.int(9,  "Start hour (New York)",   minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = grpT)
startM = input.int(30, "Start minute",            minval = 0, maxval = 59, group = grpT)
endH   = input.int(16, "Stop hour (New York)",    minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = grpT)
endM   = input.int(0,  "Stop minute",             minval = 0, maxval = 59, group = grpT)
flatten = input.bool(false, "Flatten at the window end", group = grpT,
     tooltip = "OFF by default. A fixed-time flatten cost about HALF the per-trade edge on a " +
     "sibling rule when there was no entry window, because it truncates exactly the trades a " +
     "channel exit exists to hold. It also fills at the NEXT bar's open, not the close.")

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- series
// ATR IS ta.ema(ta.tr(true), n). `ta.atr` is Wilder's RMA and is a DIFFERENT SERIES.
atr = ta.ema(ta.tr(true), atrLen)

// Realised close-to-close volatility, then its own percentile rank. `ta.percentrank(src, n)` counts
// how many of the PREVIOUS n values are <= the current one, which is exactly the research
// construction over a window of n+1.
lr     = math.log(close / close[1])
ccVol  = math.sqrt(ta.sma(lr * lr, volLen))
volPct = ta.percentrank(ccVol, rankLen - 1)

// The entry channel EXCLUDES the current bar, so a break is possible at all.
entHi = ta.highest(high, entryLen)[1]
// The exit channel takes NO offset: this value is read at the close of bar j-1 and the order it
// writes is live during bar j, which is min(low) over j-exitLen .. j-1 -- the research definition.
exLo = ta.lowest(low, exitLen)

smult = not useAdaptive ? stopFlat : volPct <= volThresh ? stopCalm : stopFast

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- session
nyH = hour(time, "America/New_York")      // bare `hour` is EXCHANGE time (Chicago for CME)
nyM = minute(time, "America/New_York")
mod = nyH * 60 + nyM
startMod = startH * 60 + startM
endMod   = endH * 60 + endM
inWindow = not useWindow or (startMod <= endMod ? mod >= startMod and mod < endMod
     : mod >= startMod or mod < endMod)
atWindowEnd = useWindow and flatten and mod >= endMod

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- state
var float stopAnchor = na
var int   lastExitBar = -2
var int   nClosed = 0

ready = not na(entHi) and not na(atr) and atr > 0 and not na(volPct)
sigOK = ready and high > entHi

// EVERY durable write is guarded. Without the guard, mid-bar evaluation reads a `close` the
// bar-close run never sees, and `stopAnchor` is durable.
if barstate.isconfirmed
    // THIS BLOCK MUST COME FIRST. A signal may not fire on the bar a trade closed on -- the research
    // position lock forbids it -- so the exit has to be recorded BEFORE the entry test runs on the
    // same bar. Counting CLOSED TRADES rather than watching position_size[1] also catches a trade
    // that opened and stopped out inside one bar, where position_size is 0 at both closes.
    if strategy.closedtrades > nClosed
        nClosed := strategy.closedtrades
        lastExitBar := bar_index

    if strategy.position_size > 0
        if atWindowEnd
            strategy.close_all(comment = "flat")
        else
            // Working stop: the NEARER of the ATR stop and the exit channel, then capped at this
            // bar's close, because a sell stop cannot rest above the market.
            lvl = math.min(math.max(stopAnchor, exLo), close)
            strategy.exit("X", from_entry = "L", stop = lvl)
    else
        if sigOK and inWindow and bar_index > lastExitBar
            stopAnchor := close - smult * atr
            strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
            // Placed WITH the entry so the order is live during the entry bar itself.
            strategy.exit("X", from_entry = "L",
                 stop = math.min(math.max(stopAnchor, exLo), close))

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- plots
plot(entHi, "Entry channel", color.new(color.teal, 40))
plot(strategy.position_size > 0 ? exLo : na, "Exit channel", color.new(color.orange, 30))
plot(strategy.position_size > 0 ? stopAnchor : na, "ATR stop", color.new(color.red, 30),
     style = plot.style_linebr)
bgcolor(useWindow and not inWindow ? color.new(color.gray, 92) : na)
plotchar(volPct, "vol percentile", "", location.top)
